From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Oct 2026 20:40:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] CECClient: wake the callback thread when the client is destroyed

CCECClient::Process() waits in m_callbackCalls.Pop(cb, 500) and only
looks at the stop flag when that times out. ~CCECClient() calls
StopThread(), which sets the flag and then waits for the thread, so
destroying a client takes up to 500 ms. Kodi creates a client at
startup to poll for adapters, with or without one plugged in, and its
exit stalls on this for 0 to 500 ms every time.

Set the stop flag first, push a null entry so Pop() returns at once,
then wait for the thread, as CCECProcessor::Close() does with its own
buffer. Process() skips the null entry.

Upstream: https://github.com/Pulse-Eight/libcec/issues/757

---
--- a/src/libcec/CECClient.cpp
+++ b/src/libcec/CECClient.cpp
@@ -75,6 +75,9 @@
 
 CCECClient::~CCECClient(void)
 {
+  // set the stop flag, then wake Process() so it doesn't wait out its poll
+  StopThread(-1);
+  m_callbackCalls.Push(NULL);
   StopThread();
   CCallbackWrap* cb;
   while (!m_callbackCalls.IsEmpty())
@@ -1836,6 +1839,8 @@
   {
     if (m_callbackCalls.Pop(cb, 500))
     {
+      if (!cb)
+        continue;
       bool keepResult = cb->m_keepResult;
       try
       {
